WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.233
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Monday, November 23.

2
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:24.954
Three things today: President Lee reshuffles his security team and names a historic spy chief, South Korea's stock market falls sharply and the government steps in, and Kim Jong Un holds an unprecedented meeting with foreign ambassadors.

3
00:00:24.954 --> 00:00:35.302
That reshuffled security team signals a turn toward the U.S. alliance, and it comes as the market falls and Kim makes an unusually direct move in diplomacy.

4
00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:43.130
Tomorrow, the new buyback rule ease takes effect and an international conference on Korean unification opens in Seoul.

5
00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:57.617
First, the cabinet reshuffle. President Lee Jae-myung has nominated five new officials to his cabinet, and the most watched pick is former Justice Minister Kang Kum-sil for director of the National Intelligence Service.

6
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:09.326
If the National Assembly confirms her, Kang would be the first woman to lead the agency since its founding in 1961. That's the headline, but the direction of the reshuffle matters just as much.

7
00:01:09.326 --> 00:01:14.483
Presidential Chief of Staff Lim Kwang-hyun announced the changes at a press briefing.

8
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:27.223
For foreign minister, Lee tapped Lim Woong-soon, currently the Second Deputy National Security Adviser, a career diplomat with extensive experience in U.S. affairs who previously served as ambassador to Canada.

9
00:01:27.223 --> 00:01:39.357
For unification minister, he nominated former Democratic Party floor leader Hong Ihk-pyo. The reshuffle also names Han Jun-ho as interior minister nominee and Kang Yu-jung as culture minister nominee.

10
00:01:39.357 --> 00:01:51.613
Hankyoreh reported that a Blue House official framed the shake-up as aimed at redefining the National Intelligence Service's legal and institutional role rather than expanding its inter-Korean function.

11
00:01:51.613 --> 00:02:01.502
Outgoing officials were described as the "self-reliance faction." Their replacements, the reporting says, are more closely aligned with an alliance-first approach.

12
00:02:01.502 --> 00:02:14.607
That read is reinforced separately by Newsis, which noted that Lim's appointment alongside the retention of National Security Adviser Wi Sung-lac is being interpreted as a shift toward prioritizing the U.S. alliance.

13
00:02:14.607 --> 00:02:24.192
Meanwhile, Defense Minister Kang Shin-cheol appeared before the National Assembly defense committee for a plenary session, answering questions from lawmakers.

14
00:02:24.192 --> 00:02:31.230
The bottom line: a five-post reshuffle in one day, with the alliance-first signal running through nearly every pick.

15
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:44.836
Second, the market drop. South Korea's stock market fell sharply on November 22, and the government moved quickly. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed down about 3.3 percent on November 23.

16
00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:53.778
The government characterized the sell-off as driven by uncertainty over artificial intelligence and semiconductor industry profitability, U.S.

17
00:02:53.778 --> 00:03:00.516
data-center regulations, tighter semiconductor export controls, and potential U.S. interest rate increases.

18
00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:11.033
Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Hyoung Il convened an emergency market review on November 22 and announced phased stabilization measures.

19
00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:22.557
Those include preparations to activate the Stock Market Stabilization Fund and a three-month easing of daily share-repurchase order limits for listed companies, effective November 24.

20
00:03:22.557 --> 00:03:30.681
The Financial Services Commission formally approved that buyback rule change at its twentieth regular meeting today, November 23.

21
00:03:30.681 --> 00:03:41.702
Lee also said in separate remarks that growth in the high 3 percent range is achievable this year, and that 2027 economic policy will focus on what he called "growth for all."

22
00:03:41.702 --> 00:03:53.856
There is some broader context in the trade picture. South Korea's early November export data showed gains following the country's annual export total crossing the one trillion dollar milestone.

23
00:03:53.856 --> 00:04:00.090
The measures kick in tomorrow. Watch whether the KOSPI steadies once the buyback easing takes hold.

24
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:10.128
Third, Kim Jong Un and the ambassadors. Kim Jong Un personally received credentials from the ambassadors of ten countries on November 22.

25
00:04:10.128 --> 00:04:21.805
The ten countries were Russia, Sweden, Indonesia, Vietnam, Brazil, the Czech Republic, India, the Philippines, Thailand, and Bangladesh. Foreign Minister Choe Son Hui also attended.

26
00:04:21.805 --> 00:04:24.321
Kim delivered a speech at the ceremony.

27
00:04:24.321 --> 00:04:38.513
At the credentials ceremony on November 22, he said: "The security situation in North Korea and the political and military environment in East Asia urgently demand that we provide for ourselves the power and capability."

28
00:04:38.513 --> 00:04:49.932
He framed North Korea's military buildup as an exercise of sovereign self-defense and said the country pursues no hegemony or expansion. The ceremony is notable for its breadth.

29
00:04:49.932 --> 00:04:59.995
Ten ambassadors at once, in a format North Korea had not used in over three decades, signals a deliberate statement about Pyongyang's international posture.

30
00:04:59.995 --> 00:05:15.091
It comes as new satellite photographs published by the Wall Street Journal document expansion of the uranium-enrichment facility at Yongbyon, adding visual evidence to assessments of a new centrifuge hall first reported in April 2026.

31
00:05:15.091 --> 00:05:25.800
Also in the region: South Korea's Joint Chiefs confirmed they are tracking and monitoring a North Korean vessel marked "Marine Police" spotted near Yeonpyeong Island.

32
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:35.588
Now to Pyongyang. KCNA's main item today covers the same event: Kim's direct reception of foreign ambassadors' credentials on November 22.

33
00:05:35.588 --> 00:05:44.861
State media emphasized his assertion that North Korea's military buildup constitutes self-defense, with no acknowledgment of U.S. dialogue overtures.

34
00:05:44.861 --> 00:05:56.934
The other domestic item KCNA led with was an announcement that Workers' Party cell leaders will convene for a conference. The failed Trump summit offer remained entirely absent from KCNA output.

35
00:05:56.934 --> 00:06:05.460
The bottom line from state media: Pyongyang is projecting sovereign confidence outward while keeping internal party mobilization in view.

36
00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:18.292
The rest of the news, quickly. South Korea's education minister Choi Kyo-jin, 72, has resigned, saying he is too old to handle the challenge of artificial intelligence-enabled exam cheating.

37
00:06:18.292 --> 00:06:25.515
A senior judge publicly rebuked the ruling Democratic Party's push to impeach Supreme Court Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae.

38
00:06:25.515 --> 00:06:44.861
Kang Dong-won, chair of the National Conference of Judge Representatives, posted a message on the courts' internal network accusing the party of going beyond the bounds, after the party announced the impeachment push following a partial reversal of a ruling sentencing former Prime Minister Han Duck-soo to 15 years.

39
00:06:44.861 --> 00:06:53.064
South Korea is formally pursuing International Atomic Energy Agency safeguards verification for its nuclear-powered submarine program.

40
00:06:53.064 --> 00:07:02.553
Framework Convention on Climate Change Conference of Parties closed in Antalya, Turkey on November 23, two days late and without adopting a cover decision.

41
00:07:02.553 --> 00:07:11.185
South Korea's Foreign Ministry described it as the first failure to reach a comprehensive climate agreement since the 2009 Copenhagen summit.

42
00:07:11.185 --> 00:07:16.450
South Korea's delegation was led by Climate-Energy-Environment Minister Kim Sung-hwan.

43
00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:26.918
South Korea's Foreign Minister Cho Hyun met Mongolian Foreign Minister Batmunkh Battsetseg in Seoul, the first official visit by a Mongolian foreign minister in ten years.

44
00:07:26.918 --> 00:07:38.795
They discussed accelerating a Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement, expanding rare-mineral supply-chain cooperation, and Mongolia's potential role in facilitating Korean Peninsula peace.

45
00:07:38.795 --> 00:07:46.325
Korea Aerospace Industries announced a 1.5 trillion won investment in aerospace and defense sectors in the Yeongnam region.

46
00:07:46.325 --> 00:07:56.854
A Russian government order signed by Prime Minister Mikhail Mishustin on September 26 proposes a joint ecological survey of the Tumen River with North Korea and China. N.K.

47
00:07:56.854 --> 00:08:04.078
News notes the plan appears tied to Beijing's ambitions to gain access to the Sea of Japan through the Tumen corridor.

48
00:08:04.078 --> 00:08:15.159
A Gallup Korea poll released November 22 found 47 percent of respondents cited the economy as the most important issue heading into the next general election, taken November 3 to 5.

49
00:08:15.159 --> 00:08:17.730
Presidential approval stood at 41 percent.

50
00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:35.030
Tomorrow: the Financial Services Commission's buyback rule ease takes effect, and the Unification Ministry co-hosts an international conference at Seoul National University on Korean-model artificial intelligence deliberation, featuring Taiwan's former cyber ambassador Audrey Tang.

51
00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:37.217
On Capitol Hill.

52
00:08:37.217 --> 00:08:54.615
Democratic Senator Chris Coons of Delaware, speaking at the Halifax International Security Forum, warned that North Korea's troop and equipment cooperation with Russia, China, and Iran now constrains Washington's freedom to act and is producing a more capable North Korean military.

53
00:08:54.615 --> 00:09:06.460
Separately, analysts are calling on South Korea to urgently build working relationships with the new U.S. Congress, citing midterm results that produced new alliance defenders on Capitol Hill.

54
00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:08.809
Beyond the region.

55
00:09:08.809 --> 00:09:28.670
A Korean-led consortium, including Korea National Railway Corp., Sambo Engineering, and Yooshin Engineering, won the 560-billion-won feasibility study and preliminary design contract for Vietnam's North-South high-speed railway, the largest overseas rail planning and design deal South Korea has secured abroad.

56
00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:35.823
The Los Angeles Times reports Saudi Arabia is approaching South Korea and others to procure air-defense systems.

57
00:09:35.823 --> 00:09:45.210
And the Korean consortium building the Czech Dukovany nuclear plant has vowed to complete it on schedule despite a construction contractor dispute.

58
00:09:46.410 --> 00:10:01.794
Finally, the markets. The KOSPI closed at about 4,709, down about 3.3 percent on November 23. The dollar bought about 1,346 won. Brent crude fell about 4.5 percent to about 88 dollars a barrel.

59
00:10:01.794 --> 00:10:11.200
That's the Korea Daily Brief for Monday, November 23. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
